{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-132526",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071085/assets/7e0a0b1860f316f7954e85b9d7f6567d9e0c05bf316cb8af3901037abe6fd17e.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. It is a type II region, and its type II representation is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}\u20615,\\,{e}^{y}\\le x\\le 5}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the top boundary of the region, written in the form $x=x(y),$ is\n\n$$\nx={e}^{y}\\,\u27f9\\,y=\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x,\n$$\n\nand the bottom boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=0.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we write $D$ as a type I region. The type I representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 5,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}\u2061x},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071085/assets/3c95dd90ad02ab708f5893e47e7d7d0c8a212c18aed1972fb1bba1362181c383.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}\u20615}{\\int }_{{e}^{y}}^{5}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{1}^{5}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
